PREFLIGHT
{"file":"HKF-118.pdf","title":"Adaptive Partition and Resource Allocation for Collaborative Inference Services in UAV Swarm","page":2,"section":"II.D Problem Formulation","quote":"The objective of this paper is to obtain"}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Defines a cloud-edge-end multi-UAV collaborative inference model (M ESs, N UAVs, 1 cloud server, L-layer DNN, 5 partition modes) and weighted latency-energy problem P, but contains formulation inconsistencies (Eq. (3) cloud time reuses edge capacity fmlambda_{k,m}, cloud indicator ck is unused in P, and CAPRI is renamed CAPRIC in Section III.A).","evidence":[{"page":1,"section":"II. SYSTEM MODEL AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"we consider a scenario consisting of multiple ESs and UAVs."},{"page":2,"section":"II.D Problem Formulation","quote":"The objective of this paper is to obtain model partition and"},{"page":2,"section":"II.B Latency Model","quote":"For cloud server, Tc"}],"note":"Verified against original HKF-118.pdf and HKF-118-01.txt."}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Explicitly states ES compute ratio (C1), ES bandwidth ratio (C2), partition range (C3), delay/energy limits (C4), UAV frequency bounds (Eq. 9), and ignores result return time, but Section II.E reduces ES selection to the closest ES and omits how C4 limits are enforced in PPO or convex subproblems.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II.D Problem Formulation","quote":"(C4) illustrates the limited delay and energy consumption,"},{"page":2,"section":"II.E PPO-Based Online Orchestration","quote":"select the ES closest to the UAV."},{"page":2,"section":"II.B Latency Model","quote":"this paper, we ignore the return time."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D03","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"NO: Section III.A specifies only 10 UAVs, 4 ESs, 1 cloud server, and two dataset citations ([3], [4]); it omits the DNN architecture/layer count L, compute/bandwidth capacities, channel parameters, alpha values, PPO hyperparameters, and simulator/software details needed to reconstruct the evaluation.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"This experiment considers 10 UAVs, 4 ESs and one cloud"},{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"This paper adopts the public drone delivery dataset [3]."}],"note":"Checked all 3 pages; no parameter table or hyperparameter listing exists."}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D04","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Names seven baselines (local, DQN, cloud_only, no_local, edge_only, end_edge, no_energy) in Section III.A, but omits baseline hyperparameters, exact training/test conditions, and alpha range details.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"We compare the proposed algorithm with the following"},{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"2.DQN: Using the DQN algorithm, while keeping"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D05","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: PPO reward directly mirrors the negative mean weighted latency-energy objective in P, and Figs. 2-3 plot reward convergence and rewards across alpha, but separate latency, energy, and C4 constraint-satisfaction metrics are not reported.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II.E PPO-Based Online Orchestration","quote":"The reward is rt =−"},{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"Fig. 2: Reward convergence Fig. 3: Rewards on α"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D06","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Fig. 3 examines sensitivity to latency weight alpha, but no repeated trials, random seeds, dispersion metrics, or confidence intervals are reported in Section III.A.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"Fig. 3 demonstrates the rewards on α, as α increases,"}],"note":"Checked Section III.A (p. 3); no repetitions or variability statistics found."}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Incorporates a simulated UAV delivery dataset [3], real Australian base station locations [4], and a path-loss/fading term in Eq. (12), but ignores return time, fixes ES association to nearest ES, and omits handoffs, link outages, or UAV failures.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"The edge servers utilize a real dataset of base station locations"},{"page":2,"section":"II.B Latency Model","quote":"this paper, we ignore the return time."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Claims CAPRI/CAPRIC achieves a better latency-energy trade-off and outperforms all baselines, but evidence consists solely of two unquantified reward plots (Figs. 2-3) without separate latency/energy numbers, constraint checks, or statistical testing.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"the reward convergence of all algorithms, where CAPRIC"},{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"Nevertheless, CAPRIC achieves the highest reward across all configurations,"}],"note":"Note method name discrepancy: CAPRI in Abstract/Intro vs CAPRIC in Section III.A."}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D09","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"NOT_REPORTED: Checked Sections III.A, IV, and References (p. 3); only third-party datasets [3] and [4] are cited, with no author code, dataset, or artifact availability statement or URL.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"This paper adopts the public drone delivery dataset [3]."},{"page":3,"section":"IV. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS","quote":"This work was supported by National Science and Tech-"}],"note":"Checked all 3 pages; no author artifact repository reported."}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"appraisal","key":"D10","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Funding grants are disclosed in Section IV Acknowledgements (p. 3) and ignoring return time is noted in Section II.B (p. 2), but there is no limitations discussion, conclusion section, or conflict-of-interest statement.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"IV. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS","quote":"National Natural Science Foundation of China (No. 62572484),"},{"page":2,"section":"II.B Latency Model","quote":"this paper, we ignore the return time."}],"note":"Checked front matter and pp. 2-3; no conflict-of-interest or limitations section."}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"report_coverage","key":"coverage","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PLOT_ONLY: Checked Section III.A (p. 3), which contains only Fig. 2 (Reward convergence) and Fig. 3 (Rewards on alpha) with qualitative prose; zero numerical observed outcome values or results tables are printed.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"Fig. 2: Reward convergence Fig. 3: Rewards on α"},{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"Nevertheless, CAPRIC achieves the highest reward across all configurations,"}],"note":"No appendices or supplementary tables present in the 3-page paper."}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Decisions: partition layers pk, qk, ES selection mk (simplified to closest ES in II.E), cloud indicator ck (inferred from action), ES compute ratio lambda_{k,m}, ES bandwidth ratio eta_{k,m}, and UAV CPU frequency fn. Objective: minimize sum_{k=1}^K (alphaTk + Ek). Constraints: C1 sum lambda_{k,m}<=1, C2 sum eta_{k,m}<=1, C3 1<=pk<=qk<=L, C4 Tk<=T_limit and Ek<=E_limit, and f_n^min<=fn<=f_n^max. Solver: hybrid PPO for discrete partition actions plus KKT closed-form convex subproblems P1-P3 for compute ratio, UAV frequency, and bandwidth. Missing: PPO hyperparameters and C4 enforcement mechanism.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II.D Problem Formulation","quote":"The objective of this paper is to obtain model partition and"},{"page":2,"section":"II.E PPO-Based Online Orchestration","quote":"we adopt Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)."},{"page":3,"section":"II.F Analytical Resource Allocation","quote":"the optimal solution can be derived by the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions as"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"technical","key":"t2_partition","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Two cut points pk and qk partition an L-layer DNN (convolutional set V, fully connected set U) across UAV, edge server, and cloud in five modes: UAV-only, UAV+ES, UAV+ES+Cloud, ES-only, and ES+Cloud. Missing: specific DNN architecture and layer count L.","evidence":[{"page":1,"section":"II. SYSTEM MODEL AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"pk is the first partition layer point for the task"},{"page":2,"section":"II.A Inference Partitioning","quote":"There are five situations: 1. The inference service only"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"technical","key":"t2_topology","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Three-tier cloud-edge-end topology with N UAVs (10 in simulation), M edge servers (4 in simulation), and 1 cloud server; UAVs generate inference requests and run local prefix layers, closest ES runs middle layers and allocates compute/bandwidth, and cloud runs optional suffix layers. Missing: physical/logical placement of PPO orchestrator and inter-ES topology details.","evidence":[{"page":1,"section":"II. SYSTEM MODEL AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"we consider a scenario consisting of multiple ESs and UAVs."},{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"This experiment considers 10 UAVs, 4 ESs and one cloud"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"technical","key":"t2_workload","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Workload/data: generic L-layer DNN with convolutional and fully connected layers; uses public simulated drone delivery dataset [3] and real Australian base station location dataset [4]. Baselines: 7 comparators (local, DQN, cloud_only, no_local, edge_only, end_edge, no_energy). Missing: specific DNN model name, inference task dataset, request arrival rate, and feature sizes.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"This paper adopts the public drone delivery dataset [3]."},{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"1.local: Inference service is computed on UAV only."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"technical","key":"t3_network_event_response","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Models time-varying UAV-to-ES distances from mobility trajectories [3] and wireless rate via Shannon capacity with transmit power delta_n, channel gain |omega_n^m|^2, path-loss exponent mu, and noise sigma^2 (Eq. 12); adapts partition points via per-slot PPO and reallocates ES bandwidth/compute and UAV frequency via KKT formulas. Missing: explicit link outage, packet loss, handoff overhead, or UAV failure/recovery mechanisms.","evidence":[{"page":1,"section":"I. INTRODUCTION","quote":"due to the mobility of UAVs, the relative distances"},{"page":3,"section":"II.F Analytical Resource Allocation","quote":"3) Bandwidth Allocation of Edge Server:"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-118","kind":"technical","key":"t3_platform_modality","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Trace-driven simulation using the public simulated UAV delivery dataset [3] and real Australian base station location dataset [4] with 10 UAVs, 4 ESs, and 1 cloud server. Missing: simulator software, programming framework, host hardware, and numerical parameter table.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"This experiment considers 10 UAVs, 4 ESs and one cloud"},{"page":3,"section":"III.A Simulation Setup","quote":"The edge servers utilize a real dataset of base station locations"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Clearly defines the multi-UAV urban monitoring system (1 GCS, C swarms, sequentially dependent partitioned task modules), collaborative inference and migration models, LST-GNN rolling prediction, MINLP formulation decoupled into P1/P2, and PDBH algorithm.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II. SCHEMA OF PROMISE","quote":"there is a GCS located at a fixed geographical position PG and C UAV-swarms"},{"page":6,"section":"VI. PDBH-BASED SOLUTION","quote":"the PDBH algorithm stated in Algorithm 1 is designed to decompose"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Explicitly defines constraints for P1 (C1 single target swarm, C2 battery budget >= E_fly + B_min) and P2 (C1 unique UAV assignment, C2 compute capacity <= F_max, C3 deadline <= Delta_Mq), battery-swap return downtime, and failure/recovery assumptions.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"III.B UAV Migration Model","quote":"If the remaining battery level of uc,i drops to a predefined"},{"page":5,"section":"V.A Problem Formulation","quote":"C2 : Bi(t) ≥Efly"},{"page":6,"section":"V.A Problem Formulation","quote":"C3 : Ttotal"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D03","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Reports host CPU/RAM, UAV hardware specs (Fig. 2(b)), swarm topology (3 swarms of 5/10/15 UAVs in 100 m triangle), bandwidth [16,32] Mbps, compute [12,36] TOPS, ResNet-18/CIFAR-10, 2 modules, failure probability 0.0001, and Fig. 2(a) parameters, but omits LST-GNN training/hyperparameters (window Y, thresholds mu_out/mu_in/mu_s-in), battery capacity/swap time, and simulator framework.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"an Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-12600KF CPU at 3.70 GHz"},{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"The three swarms are arranged in an equilat-"},{"page":5,"section":"IV.B LST-GNN-based Prediction","quote":"within a historical window of length Y."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D04","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Evaluates Promise against four named comparators (NPG [20], THR-G [21], LSTM-P [22], DTASM [23]) under three identical task-scale scenarios (20, 40, and 80 tasks/time-slot) over 1000 time-slots.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.B Evaluation of Promise","quote":"including the No-Prediction and Greedy Migration (NPG) [20],"},{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"each swarm independently generates 20, 40, or 80 tasks per time-slot,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D05","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Evaluation metrics directly align with the formulated objectives and service-continuity goals, measuring task execution success rate (Fig. 3), total migration cost matching C_total (Fig. 4), and ResNet-18 average classification accuracy (Fig. 5).","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"V.A Problem Formulation","quote":"which can be defined as Ctotal = Cuav-mig + Cser-mig."},{"page":8,"section":"VII.B Evaluation of Promise","quote":"Success rates of task execution with different schemes under various task scales."},{"page":8,"section":"VII.B Evaluation of Promise","quote":"Migration costs of different schemes under various task scales."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D06","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Evaluates sensitivity across three task scales (20, 40, 80 tasks/time-slot) over 1000 time-slots, but does not report multiple random seeds, repeated runs, variance, or confidence intervals.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"each swarm independently generates 20, 40, or 80 tasks per time-slot,"},{"page":8,"section":"VII.B Evaluation of Promise","quote":"0 200 400 600 800 1000"}],"note":"Checked Section VII (pp. 7-8); no repetitions or variability bounds reported."}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Incorporates heterogeneous compute/bandwidth, battery-threshold return/swap downtime, and random UAV failures (0.0001/slot) informed by real UAV platform specs, but uses simulation with static swarm centers (100 m triangle), simplified fixed-rate links without wireless fading/path-loss modeling, and uninterrupted GCS connectivity.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"random UAV failures are introduced in each time-slot with a probability of 0.0001."},{"page":9,"section":"VIII. CONCLUSION","quote":"anomalies such as locating disruptions and intermittent GCS connectivity,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Section VIII claims experiments comprehensively verified effectiveness and efficiency, which is slightly broad given plot-only simulation results where Promise incurs higher migration cost than THR-G/DTASM (Fig. 4) and lower accuracy than THR-G (Fig. 5), though Section VII.B explicitly notes these trade-offs.","evidence":[{"page":9,"section":"VIII. CONCLUSION","quote":"The experiments based on real-world platform configurations comprehensively verified"},{"page":8,"section":"VII.B Evaluation of Promise","quote":"the Promise performs more frequent migrations than other schemes."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D09","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"NOT_REPORTED: Checked Sections VII.A, VIII, Acknowledgement, and References (pp. 7, 9); only public CIFAR-10/ResNet-18 and cited baseline papers are referenced, with no author code, dataset, or artifact URL.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"a ResNet-18 model trained on the CIFAR-10 dataset is adopted"},{"page":9,"section":"ACKNOWLEDGEMENT","quote":"National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grants 62572122."}],"note":"Checked all 9 pages; no author artifact repository reported."}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"appraisal","key":"D10","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Funding (NSFC Grant 62572122) is disclosed in Acknowledgement (p. 9) and future-work limitations (dynamic UAV join/exit across operators, localization disruptions, intermittent GCS connectivity) are noted in Section VIII (p. 9); no conflict-of-interest statement is present.","evidence":[{"page":9,"section":"ACKNOWLEDGEMENT","quote":"National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grants 62572122."},{"page":9,"section":"VIII. CONCLUSION","quote":"dynamic UAV joining and exiting in the scenario of different operators,"}],"note":"Checked front matter and p. 9; no conflict-of-interest declaration."}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"report_coverage","key":"coverage","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PLOT_ONLY: Checked Section VII.B (pp. 7-8); all observed results (task success rate in Fig. 3, total migration cost in Fig. 4, and average inference accuracy in Fig. 5 across 20/40/80 tasks/time-slot) are presented solely as plots with qualitative narrative comparisons and no printed numerical values or tables.","evidence":[{"page":8,"section":"VII.B Evaluation of Promise","quote":"Success rates of task execution with different schemes under various task scales."},{"page":8,"section":"VII.B Evaluation of Promise","quote":"Average inference accuracies with different schemes under various"}],"note":"No numerical outcome tables or appendices exist."}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Decisions: binary cross-swarm UAV migration y_{c,i}^{c'}(t) and intra-swarm task-module migration/scheduling x_{c,i}^{m_{q,n}}(t). Objective: minimize C_total = C_uav-mig + C_ser-mig, weighting delay (alpha=0.7) and energy (beta=0.5). Constraints: P1 single-swarm move (C1) and battery>=E_fly+B_min (C2); P2 exact module assignment (C1), compute<=F_max (C2), and deadline T_total<=Delta_Mq (C3). Solver: LST-GNN (1-layer GCN + GRU) rolling binary prediction followed by hierarchical decoupling into P1 then P2, solved via Prediction-Driven Blocked Hungarian (PDBH) on independent bipartite subgraphs. Missing: LST-GNN training details and decision thresholds.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"V.A Problem Formulation","quote":"the migration problem is formulated as a MINLP"},{"page":6,"section":"VI. PDBH-BASED SOLUTION","quote":"each of which is solved efficiently using the Hungarian algorithm."},{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"Pfly 1600 W"}],"note":"Fig. 2(a) table also lists Ptx=2 W, Prx=2 W, vc,i=5 m/s, alpha=0.7, beta=0.5."}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"technical","key":"t2_partition","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"In the system model, an inference task Mq is partitioned into N sequentially dependent modules distributed within a swarm; in experiments (Section VII.A), each ResNet-18 task is equally partitioned into 2 task modules initially distributed to different UAVs within the same swarm via a Greedy allocation algorithm, then migrated reactively/predictively via PDBH. Missing: exact ResNet-18 cut layer index.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II. SCHEMA OF PROMISE","quote":"uc,i partitions Mq into N sequentially dependent task modules"},{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"Each generated task is equally partition into two task modules,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"technical","key":"t2_topology","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Centralized-coordinated multi-swarm edge-edge topology: 1 fixed GCS at the origin coordinates 3 UAV swarms (5, 10, and 15 UAVs; 30 total) arranged in an equilateral triangle of side length 100 m. UAVs perform intra-swarm collaborative inference and cross-swarm redeployment or return to GCS for battery swaps. Missing: intra-swarm spatial geometry and GCS-to-UAV link budget.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"the system consists of a GCS located at the origin and three UAV swarms"},{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"where the numbers of UAVs in the three swarms are set to 5, 10, and 15, respectively."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"technical","key":"t2_workload","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Model/task/dataset: ResNet-18 trained on CIFAR-10 for image classification (emulating urban monitoring), generating 20, 40, or 80 tasks/time-slot independently per swarm over 1000 time-slots. Baselines: 4 comparators (NPG [20], THR-G [21], LSTM-P [22], DTASM [23]). Missing: per-module FLOPs/data size and task deadline Delta_Mq values.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"a ResNet-18 model trained on the CIFAR-10 dataset is adopted"},{"page":7,"section":"VII.B Evaluation of Promise","quote":"including the No-Prediction and Greedy Migration (NPG) [20],"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"technical","key":"t3_network_event_response","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Each UAV has heterogeneous bandwidth [16,32] Mbps and compute [12,36] TOPS; random UAV failures occur at probability 0.0001 per time-slot, and low battery (<=B_min) triggers GCS return/swap downtime. Event response combines LST-GNN predictive migration (before overload/interruption), reactive recovery migration (redistributing failed modules from source UAV), and cross-swarm UAV migration. Missing: wireless channel propagation/fading model and intermittent GCS link handling.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"communication bandwidth of [16,32] Mbps and a computing power of [12,36]"},{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"random UAV failures are introduced in each time-slot with a probability of 0.0001."},{"page":4,"section":"III.C Service Migration Model","quote":"To restore the service, the GCS performs the recovery migration,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-116","kind":"technical","key":"t3_platform_modality","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Simulation executed on a server with Intel Core i5-12600KF (3.70 GHz) and 32 GB RAM, parameterized by a real-world UAV hardware profile (S500 frame, TMotor2216, Pixhawk6C, 5400mAh 3S/6S battery, NVIDIA Orin Nano 4GB/8GB). Missing: simulator software/library, OS/ML framework, and real-world flight traces.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"simulation experiments are performed to evaluate the effectiveness"},{"page":7,"section":"VII.A Experimental Settings","quote":"Edge Processor Orin Nano 4GB/8GB"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Clearly defines the N-UAV swarm system model (leader U1 holding trajectories/channels/tasks), dual-model switching between single-UAV lightweight CNN and swarm-collaborative large CNN, latency/channel models, utility maximization, and GA-based DMS algorithm.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"III. SYSTEM MODEL","quote":"our system consists of N UAVs, denoted as UN,"},{"page":4,"section":"IV. ALGORITHM DESIGN","quote":"we present a novel approach called DMS to address the problem"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Explicitly specifies memory constraints (11), computation constraints (12), unique layer-to-UAV assignment (13), binary decision variables (14), and accuracy/latency weights summing to 1, while stating energy consumption is deferred to future work.","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"III.C Problem Formulation","quote":"The presence of constraints (11) and (12) ensures that each UAV"},{"page":4,"section":"III.C Problem Formulation","quote":"Constraint (13) implies that each layer only be executed once"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D03","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Reports embedded device specs (Raspberry Pi Zero/3B+, Odroid, Orange Pi Zero, BeagleBone AI), 50% usable RAM rule, f_n formula, CNN models (VGG16, AlexNet, SqueezeNet), and trajectory dataset [21], but omits GA hyperparameters (I, G, mutation rates), weights (lambda_1, lambda_2), channel constants (B_{i,k}, P, alpha, N_0), and simulator software.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"V.A Simulation Settings","quote":"including Raspberry Pi Zero, Raspberry Pi 3B+, Odroid, Orange Pi Zero, and BeagleBone AI."},{"page":5,"section":"V.A Simulation Settings","quote":"based on the real-world trajectory traces obtained from [21]."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D04","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Compares DMS against four baselines (GA-uniform, GA-random, Small-only, Large-only) across two model pairings (VGG16+AlexNet and VGG16+SqueezeNet) under varying inference requests and UAV counts.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"V.A Simulation Settings","quote":"we also introduced four baseline algorithms, namely GA-uniform, GA-random, Small-only, and Large-only."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D05","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Objective maximizes weighted normalized accuracy minus normalized latency (Eq. 10), and results report both utility (Figs. 2-3) and constituent average latency (s) and average accuracy (%) (Table 1); minor wording inconsistency exists between p. 3 (AlexNet top-1 84.60%) and p. 5 (top-5 accuracy metric).","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"III.C Problem Formulation","quote":"where Ar is the normalized accuracy and Lr is the normalized latency."},{"page":5,"section":"V.A Simulation Settings","quote":"The top-5 accuracy of the models is utilized as the metric"},{"page":6,"section":"Table 1","quote":"Table 1. Latency and accuracy comparsion of different algorithms."}],"note":"Page 3 cites AlexNet top-1 accuracy as 84.60%, which appears as Small-only average accuracy in Table 1(a)."}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D06","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Conducts sensitivity sweeps over number of inference requests (1-8, Fig. 2) and UAV count (4-8, Fig. 3) across two small models, but reports no repeated GA trials, random seeds, or variance/confidence intervals.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"V.B Results Analysis","quote":"under different numbers of inference requests, with a UAV quantity of 5,"},{"page":6,"section":"V.B Results Analysis","quote":"under different numbers of UAVs when there are seven inference requests."}],"note":"Checked Section V (pp. 5-7); no repetition count or dispersion reported."}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PARTIAL: Uses real-world UAV trajectory traces from Mid-Air [21] and a distance-dependent stochastic channel rate model (Eqs. 2-3), but assumes leader U1 has global knowledge of all trajectories/channels/tasks and omits UAV energy limits, packet loss, and node failures.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"III. SYSTEM MODEL","quote":"UAV U1 has the information of all the UAVs’ trajectories, channel conditions,"},{"page":5,"section":"V.A Simulation Settings","quote":"based on the real-world trajectory traces obtained from [21]."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Conclusions accurately reflect the trace-driven simulation scope, noting improved latency-accuracy trade-off over four baselines and explicitly deferring UAV energy consumption to future work.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"VI. CONCLUSIONS","quote":"The simulation results demonstrate that our proposed approach achieves low latency"},{"page":7,"section":"VI. CONCLUSIONS","quote":"In future work, we will further investigate the impact of energy consumption"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-032","kind":"appraisal","key":"D09","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"NOT_REPORTED: Checked Sections V.A, VI, Acknowledgement, and References (pp. 5, 7); only third-party Mid-Air dataset [21] is cited, with no author code or artifact repository link.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"V.A Simulation Settings","quote":"based on the real-world trajectory traces obtained from [21]."},{"page":7,"section":"ACKNOWLEDGEMENT","quote":"National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant No. 61931011"}],"note":"Checked all 7 pages; no author artifact repository reported."}
